   "subject": "problem with mirtCAT function",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello Phil,<br><br>I fitted a multidimensional GPCM to my data using the mirt package, I estimated factor scores a first time. Then, in order to have complete response patterns (to perform CAT simualtions), I imputed the data and refitted the same model. Then I could run the mirtCAT function with all these outputs (here I generate a CAT using the first observation of my imputed data). Here is the corresponding script:<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">mm </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;gpcm&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> method </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;MHRM&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  technical </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">MAXQUAD </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">20000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> NCYCLES </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>scores </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> fscores</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> method </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;MAP&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> full</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">scores </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><br><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\">dat_imput </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> imputeMissing</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">scores</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span></code><br><br>mm2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dat_imput</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;gpcm&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> method </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;MHRM&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> technical </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">MAXQUAD </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">20000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> NCYCLES </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>mirtCAT</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mirt_object </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mm2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> method </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;MAP&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> criteria </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;Drule&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> start_item </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> 1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> local_pattern </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dat_imput</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>My problem is that I got the following error message after running the mirtCAT function:<br><br><b>&quot;Error in FI(extract.item(MCE$test$mirt_object, x), Theta = thetas) : Fisher-information matrix not currently supported for supplied classes&quot;</b><br><br>Replacing the item selection criterion by &quot;KL&quot; solves the problem, but I would like to test different criteria and compare them to define the best way to administer my CAT. How can I use the information-based criteria ?<br><br>With the &quot;KL&quot; criterion, all the items are admnistered to reach the default 0.3 threshold for the SEM (which is not reached for all the dimensions), is there a reasonable standard in the literature for the SEM threshold ?<br><br>Hung &amp; al (<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/23749433\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/23749433&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792475981033000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3oPCor8tozPBziwzvO2fzf\">http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/23749433</a>) proposes the value 3.3 as threshold but it seems weird given the SE take the values 1 at the first step of theta estimation....<br><br>Thank you for your help,<br><br>Best regards,<br><br>Pierre Michel.<br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
